<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mustapha (Bob Azzam)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded version, released in France</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mustafa (Leo Leandros)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Soldaterkammerater på vagt</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Danish version, sung in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mustafà català (Rudy Ventura)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Folk, Blues and Beyond (Davy Graham)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1964</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental version on the album</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">White Summer/Black Mountainside (Jimmy Page)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in, after Davy Graham's version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ya Mustafa (Nil Burak)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1975</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recorded version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mana Tahan (Warkop)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1979</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mustapha (Dick Lee)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">version in English and Singaporean dialects</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Aatish (Nadeem-Shravan)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same music, in the film's soundtrack</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Al Ahly and Zamalek football chants</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same rhythm and melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lebanese protest chant against Gebran Bassil</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2019</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune as the football chant</span></li></ul></section>
